1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,592
If you are choosing between ceramic and steel skate bearings, you will know which one to buy by the end of this.

2
00:00:07,592 --> 00:00:09,693
Here is the short answer first.

3
00:00:09,693 --> 00:00:12,880
If you race seriously, ceramic earns its price.

4
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:18,167
It spins up faster, runs cooler and holds its speed deeper into a long effort.

5
00:00:18,167 --> 00:00:25,421
If you train most days, or you want the most speed for your money, a precision steel set is the better buy.

6
00:00:25,421 --> 00:00:27,929
It also leaves you budget for wheels.

7
00:00:27,929 --> 00:00:30,844
For most skaters, that is the whole answer.

8
00:00:30,844 --> 00:00:39,114
The rest explains why, what ceramic really means on the box, how much speed is on the table, and how to keep any set fast.

9
00:00:39,814 --> 00:00:45,326
Every push you make has to travel through your bearings before it reaches the ground.

10
00:00:45,326 --> 00:00:48,373
Lower friction means more roll for each stride.

11
00:00:48,373 --> 00:00:50,707
It means less energy wasted as heat.

12
00:00:50,707 --> 00:00:54,792
And it means a bearing that stays fast deeper into a long race.

13
00:00:54,792 --> 00:00:57,840
A single four wheel skate holds eight bearings.

14
00:00:57,840 --> 00:00:59,201
A pair holds sixteen.

15
00:00:59,201 --> 00:01:03,092
Every one of them is turning for as long as you are rolling.

16
00:01:03,092 --> 00:01:06,139
How they perform comes down to two things only.

17
00:01:06,139 --> 00:01:09,900
How the bearing was built, and how well you look after it.

18
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,364
You control the second one completely.

19
00:01:12,364 --> 00:01:15,736
Keep that in mind, because it comes back at the end.

20
00:01:16,436 --> 00:01:18,735
Here is a bearing in cross section.

21
00:01:18,735 --> 00:01:21,231
The outer blue ring is the outer race.

22
00:01:21,231 --> 00:01:26,617
The grey ring near the middle is the inner race, around the eight millimetre bore.

23
00:01:26,617 --> 00:01:28,653
Between them, seven balls roll.

24
00:01:28,653 --> 00:01:33,448
The green dashed line is the cage, which keeps those balls evenly spaced.

25
00:01:33,448 --> 00:01:39,097
A well made cage positions each ball accurately, which cuts friction and extends life.

26
00:01:39,097 --> 00:01:40,936
The orange line is the seal.

27
00:01:40,936 --> 00:01:46,782
It keeps dirt and water away from the polished surfaces that let the bearing spin freely.

28
00:01:46,782 --> 00:01:55,453
The quality of each of these parts, and how precisely they are made and finished, is what separates a fast bearing from a cheap one.

29
00:01:56,153 --> 00:01:59,668
Before you think about materials, check your size.

30
00:01:59,668 --> 00:02:03,464
Almost every inline speed wheel takes the 608 bearing.

31
00:02:03,464 --> 00:02:10,845
That is eight millimetres on the bore, twenty two millimetres on the outside, and seven millimetres wide.

32
00:02:10,845 --> 00:02:13,024
Quad skaters are the exception.

33
00:02:13,024 --> 00:02:16,399
Many quad hubs use the smaller 627 bore instead.

34
00:02:16,399 --> 00:02:22,585
The two are not interchangeable, so confirm which one your wheels take before you order.

35
00:02:22,585 --> 00:02:28,630
A standard pair of four wheel inline skates needs sixteen bearings, two in each wheel.

36
00:02:28,630 --> 00:02:35,801
Ordering the wrong bore is the most expensive mistake you can make here, and the easiest one to avoid.

37
00:02:36,501 --> 00:02:37,900
Now the big question.

38
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:41,497
Ceramic balls are lighter and harder than steel balls.

39
00:02:41,497 --> 00:02:46,094
Because they are lighter, they spin up faster and carry less inertia.

40
00:02:46,094 --> 00:02:50,757
Because they are harder and smoother, they run cooler and last longer.

41
00:02:50,757 --> 00:02:52,756
You can see that in the chart.

42
00:02:52,756 --> 00:02:58,818
The blue ceramic bars lead on spin up speed, on staying cool under load, and on durability.

43
00:02:58,818 --> 00:03:00,483
But look at the last row.

44
00:03:00,483 --> 00:03:04,280
On value for money, the grey steel bar is clearly longer.

45
00:03:04,280 --> 00:03:08,078
Good steel bearings still roll beautifully and cost less.

46
00:03:08,078 --> 00:03:10,609
The bars are indicative, not measured.

47
00:03:10,609 --> 00:03:13,407
The point is that neither choice is wrong.

48
00:03:13,407 --> 00:03:16,272
The right one depends on how much you race.

49
00:03:16,971 --> 00:03:21,431
Here is the part almost nobody explains before you spend the money.

50
00:03:21,431 --> 00:03:26,690
When a skate bearing is sold as ceramic, it nearly always means hybrid ceramic.

51
00:03:26,690 --> 00:03:31,682
That is the bearing on the left, with ceramic balls running in steel races.

52
00:03:31,682 --> 00:03:36,009
The one on the right is full ceramic, with ceramic races as well.

53
00:03:36,009 --> 00:03:40,202
That is a different thing, and it is not what skate shops sell.

54
00:03:40,202 --> 00:03:41,866
This matters in two ways.

55
00:03:41,866 --> 00:03:46,526
First, the steel races can still rust if you put your skates away wet.

56
00:03:46,526 --> 00:03:49,388
Ceramic does not make a bearing rust proof.

57
00:03:49,388 --> 00:03:55,844
Second, the bearing still needs light oil, because the races and the cage still need lubrication.

58
00:03:55,844 --> 00:03:59,838
A ceramic bearing run dry is just an expensive slow bearing.

59
00:04:00,538 --> 00:04:04,237
You will see two ceramic materials in skate bearings.

60
00:04:04,237 --> 00:04:06,261
The first is silicon nitride.

61
00:04:06,261 --> 00:04:11,635
It is prized for elite speed, because it is extremely hard, light and smooth.

62
00:04:11,635 --> 00:04:13,240
The second is zirconia.

63
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:20,220
It trades a little outright speed for toughness, which makes it the more forgiving way into ceramic.

64
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:26,989
In the LiveLica range, the Swiss Ceramic uses silicon nitride and the Gold Ceramic uses zirconia.

65
00:04:26,989 --> 00:04:29,083
Both still sit in steel races.

66
00:04:29,083 --> 00:04:34,597
So everything you just heard about oil and dry storage applies to both of them.

67
00:04:35,297 --> 00:04:39,766
Less than the marketing implies, and more than the sceptics claim.

68
00:04:39,766 --> 00:04:48,974
The maker of one top ceramic set states a saving of zero point eight to one watt per bearing, against a typical precision steel bearing.

69
00:04:48,974 --> 00:04:56,828
Across the sixteen bearings in a pair, that is roughly thirteen to sixteen watts, on the manufacturer's own figures.

70
00:04:56,828 --> 00:04:59,401
That is a real number over a marathon.

71
00:04:59,401 --> 00:05:03,328
It is a much smaller one over a five hundred metre sprint.

72
00:05:03,328 --> 00:05:04,614
Read it in context.

73
00:05:04,614 --> 00:05:09,422
Those figures come from controlled conditions, not a dusty Indian road.

74
00:05:09,422 --> 00:05:17,953
Lower rotating weight is the part most skaters actually notice, and they notice it most in the first few strides off the line.

75
00:05:18,653 --> 00:05:21,368
Two more numbers come up when you shop.

76
00:05:21,368 --> 00:05:23,039
The first is ball count.

77
00:05:23,039 --> 00:05:25,127
Most bearings use seven balls.

78
00:05:25,127 --> 00:05:26,032
Some use six.

79
00:05:26,032 --> 00:05:32,576
Fewer balls means fewer points of contact, which reduces friction and can raise top end speed.

80
00:05:32,576 --> 00:05:36,057
Slightly larger balls keep strength and life high.

81
00:05:36,057 --> 00:05:39,398
It suits experienced racers with a clean stride.

82
00:05:39,398 --> 00:05:41,487
The second is the ABEC rating.

83
00:05:41,487 --> 00:05:45,316
ABEC is a rating of manufacturing tolerance, not speed.

84
00:05:45,316 --> 00:05:49,284
A higher number means tighter precision, which is useful.

85
00:05:49,284 --> 00:05:54,018
But it says nothing about ball material, cage design or lubrication.

86
00:05:54,018 --> 00:05:58,612
Those have a bigger effect on how a bearing feels under your feet.

87
00:05:58,612 --> 00:06:01,675
Treat ABEC as one clue, not the whole story.

88
00:06:02,375 --> 00:06:03,749
So how do you choose?

89
00:06:03,749 --> 00:06:09,242
If you train most days and want the best value, start with a high quality steel set.

90
00:06:09,242 --> 00:06:12,969
If you race and want every advantage, move up to ceramic.

91
00:06:12,969 --> 00:06:16,827
If you are new to ceramic, zirconia is the softest landing.

92
00:06:16,827 --> 00:06:20,620
If you are a beginner, steel is usually the smarter spend.

93
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:28,729
Until your technique and fitness are what hold you back, good steel gives you most of the feel for a fraction of the outlay.

94
00:06:28,729 --> 00:06:31,802
Spend the money you save on wheels or coaching.

95
00:06:31,802 --> 00:06:35,922
Move to ceramic when your kit becomes the limit, not your legs.

96
00:06:36,622 --> 00:06:39,280
Now the free speed most skaters ignore.

97
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:44,663
Clean, well oiled bearings roll faster and last far longer than neglected ones.

98
00:06:44,663 --> 00:06:50,864
A bearing that has gone gritty and dry gives speed back faster than any upgrade can buy it.

99
00:06:50,864 --> 00:06:53,999
Dirt and water are the enemies of a fast spin.

100
00:06:53,999 --> 00:06:58,496
Wipe your bearings down, keep them dry, and re oil them regularly.

101
00:06:58,496 --> 00:07:05,787
Re oil whenever the spin starts to feel slow or noisy, and always after skating in wet or dirty conditions.

102
00:07:05,787 --> 00:07:09,672
A light, regular routine beats an occasional heavy clean.

103
00:07:09,672 --> 00:07:12,057
And never put your skates away wet.

104
00:07:12,057 --> 00:07:19,757
That is the mistake that quietly ruins a ceramic set, because the races are steel whatever the balls are made of.

105
00:07:20,457 --> 00:07:22,783
So here is the one idea to remember.

106
00:07:22,783 --> 00:07:25,755
Buy steel and clean it, and you will be quick.

107
00:07:25,755 --> 00:07:29,374
Buy ceramic and still clean it, and you will be quicker.

108
00:07:29,374 --> 00:07:33,251
Skip the cleaning either way, and you have wasted the money.

109
00:07:33,251 --> 00:07:40,488
If your bearings feel gritty right now, cleaning them will do more for your lap times than anything you can buy.

110
00:07:40,488 --> 00:07:47,724
The full guide, with a comparison of four bearing sets and answers to common questions, is on the LiveLica blog.

111
00:07:47,724 --> 00:07:49,727
The link is in the description.
